        "answer": {
          "short": "Intercepts $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$; local minimum $\\left(\\frac12,-\\frac{27}{16}\\right)$; horizontal point of inflection $(2,0)$. Both ends rise.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}x(x-2)^3&=0\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe graph crosses the axes at $(0,0)$ and crosses the $x$-axis with a horizontal tangent at $(2,0)$. Its leading term is $x^4$, so $y\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to\\pm\\infty$. Draw the curve decreasing to the local minimum $\\left(\\frac12,-\\frac{27}{16}\\right)$, then increasing through the horizontal point of inflection $(2,0)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p22-q13-graph",
